# Overview
#
# A wordy phrase is a stock phrase that has a shorter form (`a number of
# flags` for `several flags`, `with respect to` for `relative to`). The
# reader reads several words for the meaning of one or two. The fix
# writes the short form. The rule sits at Recommended, because each
# phrase here also has a literal sense that the short form does not
# carry. The message tells the writer to keep the phrase in that sense.
#
# False Positives
#
# The rule still reports the literal sense of each phrase. `in terms of`
# names a unit (`the cost is expressed in terms of bytes`), and `with
# respect to` names a variable (`the derivative with respect to x`).
#
# `on the order of` stays out, because it also names a sequence (`depend
# on the order of the members`).
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Recommended ProseWordy holds `a number of`, `in terms of`, and the
# other phrases that also have a literal sense, which the short form
# does not carry. The Required ProseWordy holds every other wordy
# phrase.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 124 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 17 of 20 sampled
# hits read better in the short form. In the 3 misfires, `a number of`
# counted something (`a number of seconds`, `a number of rows`), as it
# does in `Read returns a number of bytes`. The message tells the writer
# to keep those.
expr main [(a number of), (in terms of), (with respect to), (in relation to), (in light of), (by means of), (in such a way that), (in a manner that)]
let message "Wordy. Write the short form, such as `several` for `a number of` or `relative to` for `with respect to`. Keep the phrase when it names an exact count or a unit, as in `a number of seconds`."
let description "Flags `a number of`, `in terms of`, `with respect to`, and similar phrases that most sentences can replace with one word."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "Many flags take a value."
allows "The limit counts bytes."
